body:
**Shape.** An owned local that is still owned at a `throw` site is not disposed by the unwind when a *later* statement of the same body is a binding transfer of it.

```bezel
module Probe;

public sealed class Q
  private _id: int;
  public constructor(id: int)
    _id := id;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
end Q;

public sealed class R: Exception
  private _id: int;
  public owned f: Q;
  public constructor(id: int, owned q: Q)
    base("R");
    _id := id;
    f := q;
  end constructor;
  destructor
    Console.WriteLine($"D:{_id}");
  end destructor;
  public method Use()
    Console.WriteLine($"U:{_id}");
  end Use;
  public static method Take(owned r: R)
    using var k := move r;
  end Take;
  public static method Body(flag: bool)
    var a := new R(2, new Q(1));
    if flag then
      throw new R(4, new Q(3));       // the exception edge must dispose `a` here (K3.15); it does not
    end;
    var b := move a;                  // a LATER binding transfer of `a` switches the unwind disposal above off (an owned-argument `Take(move a)` does not)
  end Body;
end R;

public class Program
  public static method Main(args: Array<string>): int
    try
      R.Body(args.Count > 0);
    catch
      var e: R => Console.WriteLine("caught"),
    end;
    Console.WriteLine("END");
    return 0;
  end Main;
end Program;
```

**Measured on r35.** Both modes. No throw: `D:2 D:1 END` (the transfer's new holder `b` disposes at scope end — correct). Throw: `caught D:4 D:3 END` — **`a` (R#2/Q#1) is never disposed**; only the caught exception is. The same with the exception left uncaught. Controls: with `a.Use();` in place of the transfer the throwing path prints `D:2 D:1` before `caught`; with an owned-argument transfer `R.Take(move a);` in place of `var b := move a;` the throwing path also prints `D:2 D:1`. So the binding-transfer form (K3.4) later in the body is what switches the earlier unwind disposal off — presumably the transfer retargets `a`'s cleanup to `b`'s slot, which is not established on the throwing path.

**Spec (v0.345).** K3.15 (§28.3.1): *"The exception edge performs the same implicit disposal as the normal edge, on the single establishment-ordered cleanup stack"*; K3.4: the post-state moved-from begins **at** the transfer — at the `throw` site `a` is owned and undischarged, so the unwind disposes it (K3.14 + K3.15). Expected on the throwing path: `D:2 D:1` then `caught`.

**Differential numbers.** 35 of 6,000 sampled programs in the all-features `--if` sample (vars 3 / len 4) are level-2 trace disagreements of this shape (a throwing arm before a later `var b := move a;` / `using var b := move a;` / `b := move a;` of a still-owned local); every one leaks the local on the throwing path and traces identically on the other path.

**Measured on r35** (`bezelc 0.0.0-f6863e62`, the 2026-10-27 release build; main may have moved since). Found by the model-vs-compiler differential (`scratch/bzdiff`, proposal 0047 §5.3 / soundness README §3.2): the executable ownership model (bzcore L1, spec v0.345) and `bezelc` disagree on this program; the model's verdict is the spec's sentence quoted above.
